  "theory": {
    "intro": "When the derivative of the inner function appears as a factor, the integral collapses back to a single trigonometric function. So $\\int f'(x)\\cos f(x)\\,dx = \\sin f(x) + C$. Without that factor the method does not apply.",
    "facts": [
      "The **inner function** $f(x)$ is the expression sitting inside the trigonometric function.",
      "$\\int f'(x)\\sin f(x)\\,dx = -\\cos f(x) + C$, keeping the minus sign from $\\int\\sin x\\,dx$.",
      "$\\int f'(x)\\cos f(x)\\,dx = \\sin f(x) + C$.",
      "$\\int f'(x)\\sec^{2}f(x)\\,dx = \\tan f(x) + C$.",
      "A constant multiple of $f'(x)$ is enough: put the matching fraction outside the integral.",
      "$\\int\\cos^{n}x\\sin x\\,dx$ fits the reverse chain rule with inner function $\\cos x$, since $f'(x) = -\\sin x$.",
      "The method fails when $f'(x)$ is missing, so $\\int\\sin\\left(x^{2}\\right)dx$ cannot be found this way.",
      "Check every answer by differentiating it with the chain rule.",
      "For a definite integral, evaluate the primitive at the given limits without changing them."
    ],
    "steps": [
      "Choose the inner function $f(x)$ and differentiate it to get $f'(x)$.",
      "Check that $f'(x)$ appears as a factor, up to a constant multiple.",
      "Write the fraction needed outside the integral so the factor inside is exactly $f'(x)$.",
      "Replace the trigonometric function by its primitive, copying $f(x)$ unchanged, and add $+C$."
    ]
  },
